powerpagecomponent.content:
{ "source": "{% comment %}
 Shared review worklist (PP-07, PP-08). Include with:
 (% include 'OT Review List' reviewtype: 120910200 heading: 'Tax reviews' %)

 al_reviewtype values are 120910200 Tax and 120910201 AQS.
 A single al_ReviewInstance carries reviewType rather than separate
 Tax and AQS tables, so both pages share this template.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
 `integer` is the only safe coercion here. `times: 1` repeats a string rather than
 converting it, so page_number became a string collection and the comparison below
 threw. `default` cannot rescue that - it only substitutes null.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign page_size = 25 %}
{% assign page_number = request.params['page'] | integer | default: 1 %}
{% if page_number < 1 %}{% assign page_number = 1 %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
 Scope (AD-218, AR-02, AR-03). A reviewer's list is their own reviews and nothing else.
 This is presentation; the boundary is the table permission, which since AD-218 gives a
 reviewer read on their own cases and the AQS queue only. So "All" is gone: it would
 have listed exactly the same rows under a label that promised more.
{% endcomment %}
{% if user == null %}
 {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'Sign in to see your reviews' message: 'This page lists the reviews allocated to you.' %}
{% else %}
{% comment %}
 Case reference search (project owner, 2026-09-10). Filtered in the query rather than over
 the rendered rows: this list pages server-side, so a client-side filter would search the
 page the checker happens to be on and quietly miss the case they were looking for.

 The condition names the linked case by alias, which is what the case list already does
 for its outcome filter - and it makes the join behave as an inner one, so a review whose
 case is missing drops out of a search rather than surviving it as a blank row.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign f_ref = request.params['ref'] | default: '' %}

{% comment %}
 Review route filter (project owner, 2026-09-10). The routes are read from the environment
 rather than listed here: al_reviewroute holds three rows whose ids are the environment's
 own, so hard-coding them would break the moment this ran anywhere but DEV - the same
 reason DeriveRoute looks a route up by its code instead of by id.

 The parameter is validated against the ids just fetched, which is this site's rule for
 anything that reaches FetchXML: an unrecognised value becomes no filter rather than a
 condition, so nothing typed into the URL is interpolated into the query.
{% endcomment %}
{% fetchxml routes %}
<fetch>
 <entity name="al_reviewroute">
 <attribute name="al_reviewrouteid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <order attribute="al_name" />
 </entity>
</fetch>
{% endfetchxml %}

{% assign f_route = request.params['route'] | default: '' %}
{% assign route_ok = false %}
{% if f_route != '' %}
 {% for r in routes.results.entities %}
 {% if r.al_reviewrouteid == f_route %}{% assign route_ok = true %}{% endif %}
 {% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% unless route_ok %}{% assign f_route = '' %}{% endunless %}

{% capture scope_qs %}{% if f_ref != '' %}ref={{ f_ref | url_escape }}&amp;{% endif %}{% if f_route != '' %}route={{ f_route | url_escape }}&amp;{% endif %}{% endcapture %}

{% fetchxml reviews %}
<fetch count="{{ page_size }}" page="{{ page_number }}" returntotalrecordcount="true">
 <entity name="al_reviewinstance">
 <attribute name="al_reviewinstanceid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <attribute name="al_reviewstatus" />
 <attribute name="al_sequence" />
 <attribute name="al_startedon" />
 <attribute name="al_submittedon" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="al_reviewtype" operator="eq" value="{{ reviewtype }}" />
 <condition attribute="al_assignedcontactid" operator="eq" value="{{ user.id | xml_escape }}" />
 {% comment %}A removed case is out of checking, so its review is nobody's work.{% endcomment %}
 <condition entityname="case" attribute="al_casestatus" operator="ne" value="120910592" />
 {% if f_ref != '' %}<condition entityname="case" attribute="al_casereference" operator="like" value="%{{ f_ref | xml_escape }}%" />{% endif %}
 {% if f_route != '' %}<condition entityname="case" attribute="al_reviewrouteid" operator="eq" value="{{ f_route | xml_escape }}" />{% endif %}
 </filter>
 <order attribute="al_startedon" descending="true" />
 <link-entity name="al_outcomecase" from="al_outcomecaseid" to="al_outcomecaseid" link-type="outer" alias="case">
 <attribute name="al_outcomecaseid" />
 <attribute name="al_casereference" />
 <attribute name="al_clientname" />
 <attribute name="al_advisername" />
 <attribute name="al_taxcheckername" />
 <attribute name="al_aqscheckername" />
 <attribute name="al_duedate" />
 {% comment %}
 The route is the case's, so it is read through the case rather than off the review.
 Outer, because the link above is: a case with no route yet is still a case, and an
 inner join here would drop its reviews off the list entirely.
 {% endcomment %}
 <link-entity name="al_reviewroute" from="al_reviewrouteid" to="al_reviewrouteid" link-type="outer" alias="route">
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 </link-entity>
 </link-entity>
 </entity>
</fetch>
{% endfetchxml %}

{% assign rows = reviews.results.entities %}

{% comment %}
 The shared queue (AD-076, extending AD-040, BR-003), AQS only (AD-113). An AQS checker
 takes a waiting case themselves rather than waiting to be allocated.

 The queue is what CaseAccessReconciler put there (AD-218): the case carries the AQS Team
 account while it waits, and the Case - AQS queue permission is how a reviewer reads it.
 Ordered by the time it joined, oldest first (AR-03). No review is joined: the queue
 permission deliberately has no children, and a queued Tax-then-AQS case is exactly one
 whose route requires Tax, because the reconciler queues it only once its Tax check is in.

 Nothing here is a permission boundary. ClaimCasePlugin re-reads the status and the route
 server-side and refuses a claim this list should not have offered (NFR-SEC-01).
{% endcomment %}
{% assign queued_status = 120910583 %}
{% assign is_aqs_queue = false %}
{% if reviewtype == 120910201 %}{% assign is_aqs_queue = true %}{% endif %}

{% if is_aqs_queue and user %}
 {% comment %}
 The queue is readable before it is actionable. Picking a case up depends on
 ClaimCasePlugin being registered; without it the Web API create still succeeds, because
 al_name, al_caseassignmentcode, al_assignedon and al_isactive are only *business*
 required and Dataverse does not enforce that through the API. The page would report
 success while the case stayed in the queue, leaving assignment rows that AD-037 forbids
 deleting. So the button is switched on by a site setting, not by deployment.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% assign claim_enabled = false %}
 {% if settings['OutcomeTesting/Claim/Enabled'] == 'true' %}{% assign claim_enabled = true %}{% endif %}

 {% comment %}
 Picking a case up needs the AQS reviewer web role: the claim-request contact permission
 is bound to the Tax and AQS Reviewer roles, and ClaimCasePlugin checks the same role
 again. A signed-in user without it - a supervisor or an adviser reading the queue, now
 that page.reviews reaches them - used to be offered "Run checks" and answered with a 403
 read as "you may not have permission". The server stays the boundary (NFR-SEC-01); this
 only stops the page offering an action it knows will be refused, and says why instead.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% assign claim_role = 'AL Portal - AQS Reviewer' %}
 {% assign can_claim = false %}
 {% if claim_enabled and user.roles contains claim_role %}{% assign can_claim = true %}{% endif %}

 {% fetchxml available %}
 <fetch count="50">
 <entity name="al_outcomecase">
 <attribute name="al_outcomecaseid" />
 <attribute name="al_casereference" />
 <attribute name="al_clientname" />
 <attribute name="al_advisername" />
 <attribute name="al_taxcheckername" />
 <attribute name="al_aqscheckername" />
 <attribute name="al_duedate" />
 <attribute name="al_aqsqueuedon" />
 <attribute name="createdon" />
 <attribute name="al_preorpostcheck" />
 <attribute name="al_preorpostcheckid" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="statecode" operator="eq" value="0" />
 <condition attribute="al_aqsqueueaccountid" operator="not-null" />
 </filter>
 <link-entity name="al_reviewroute" from="al_reviewrouteid" to="al_reviewrouteid" link-type="outer" alias="route">
 <attribute name="al_requirestaxreview" />
 </link-entity>
 <order attribute="al_aqsqueuedon" />
 </entity>
 </fetch>
 {% endfetchxml %}

 {% assign unassigned = available.results.entities %}

 {% comment %}
 Two groups, Tax-reviewed first (AR-03): a case that has already had a Tax check has
 usually been in OTIS longest, so it is flagged and put ahead of new work. Each group is
 oldest first because the fetch is. Days in OTIS counts from import; Waiting for AQS from
 the moment the reconciler queued it. Both are working days, drawn by the script at the
 end of this template on the same calendar as the remediation clock.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% for pass in (1..2) %}
 {% if pass == 1 %}
 {% assign want_tax = true %}
 {% assign q_title = 'Tax review completed – awaiting allocation' %}
 {% assign q_id = 'ot-queue-tax' %}
 {% assign q_empty = 'No case whose Tax review is complete is waiting for an AQS checker.' %}
 {% else %}
 {% assign want_tax = false %}
 {% assign q_title = 'New – awaiting allocation' %}
 {% assign q_id = 'ot-queue-new' %}
 {% assign q_empty = 'No new case is waiting for an AQS checker.' %}
 {% endif %}
 {% assign q_count = 0 %}
 {% for c in unassigned %}
 {% assign c_tax = false %}
 {% if c['route.al_requirestaxreview'] == true %}{% assign c_tax = true %}{% endif %}
 {% if c_tax == want_tax %}{% assign q_count = q_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}
 {% endfor %}

 <section class="ot-card" aria-labelledby="{{ q_id }}">
 <h2 class="ot-card__title" id="{{ q_id }}">{{ q_title }}</h2>

 {% if q_count > 0 %}
 <div class="ot-table-wrap">
 <table class="ot-table">
 <caption>
 {{ q_count }} case{% if q_count != 1 %}s{% endif %}, oldest first.{% if can_claim %} Running checks assigns the case to you.{% elsif claim_enabled %} Running checks needs the AQS Reviewer role, which your account does not hold.{% endif %}
 </caption>
 <thead>
 <tr>
 <th scope="col">Case reference</th>
 <th scope="col">Client</th>
 <th scope="col">Adviser</th>
 <th scope="col">Tax checker</th>
 <th scope="col">AQS checker</th>
 <th scope="col">Days in OTIS</th>
 <th scope="col">Waiting for AQS</th>
 <th scope="col">Due</th>
 {% if can_claim %}<th scope="col"><span class="ot-visually-hidden">Action</span></th>{% endif %}
 </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody>
 {% for c in unassigned %}
 {% assign c_tax = false %}
 {% if c['route.al_requirestaxreview'] == true %}{% assign c_tax = true %}{% endif %}
 {% if c_tax == want_tax %}
 <tr>
 <td>
 <a href="/case-details?id={{ c.al_outcomecaseid }}">
 {{ c.al_casereference | default: 'Untitled case' | escape }}
 </a>
 {% if c_tax %}<span class="ot-badge">Tax reviewed</span>{% endif %}
 {% if c.al_preorpostcheck.value == 120910541 or c.al_preorpostcheckid.name == 'Post' %}<span class="ot-badge">Post-sale</span>{% endif %}
 </td>
 <td>{{ c.al_clientname | escape }}</td>
 <td>{{ c.al_advisername | escape }}</td>
 {% capture tax_checker %}{{ c.al_taxcheckername }}{% endcapture %}
 {% capture aqs_checker %}{{ c.al_aqscheckername }}{% endcapture %}
 <td>{% if tax_checker != '' %}{{ tax_checker | escape }}{% else %}<span class="ot-checker">Not yet allocated</span>{% endif %}</td>
 <td>{% if aqs_checker != '' %}{{ aqs_checker | escape }}{% else %}<span class="ot-checker">Not yet allocated</span>{% endif %}</td>
 <td><span data-ot-workdays data-from="{{ c.createdon | date: 'yyyy-MM-dd' }}">—</span></td>
 <td><span data-ot-workdays data-from="{{ c.al_aqsqueuedon | date: 'yyyy-MM-dd' }}">—</span></td>
 <td>{{ c.al_duedate | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' | default: '—' }}</td>
 {% if can_claim %}
 <td>
 <button type="button"
 class="ot-btn ot-btn--primary"
 data-ot-claim
 data-case-id="{{ c.al_outcomecaseid }}"
 data-case-reference="{{ c.al_casereference | default: 'this case' | escape }}">
 Run checks
 </button>
 </td>
 {% endif %}
 </tr>
 {% endif %}
 {% endfor %}
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>
 {% else %}
 {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'Nothing waiting' message: q_empty %}
 {% endif %}
 </section>
 {% endfor %}
 {% if can_claim %}<p class="ot-response__status" data-ot-claim-status aria-live="polite"></p>{% endif %}

 {% if can_claim %}
 <dialog class="ot-dialog" data-ot-claim-dialog aria-labelledby="ot-claim-dialog-title">
 <h2 class="ot-dialog__title" id="ot-claim-dialog-title">Start checks on this case?</h2>
 <p>
 You are about to start checks on <strong data-ot-claim-dialog-case></strong>. The case will be
 assigned to you and will no longer be available for anyone else to pick up. The checklist
 opens straight away so you can start.
 </p>
 <p class="ot-dialog__actions">
 <button type="button" class="ot-btn" data-ot-claim-cancel>Cancel</button>
 <button type="button" class="ot-btn ot-btn--primary" data-ot-claim-continue>Continue</button>
 </p>
 </dialog>
 {% endif %}
{% endif %}


{% comment %}
 Case reference search. A GET form, so a search is a URL a checker can bookmark or send to
 someone, and so paging keeps it. The scope is carried in a hidden field for the same
 reason the links above carry the search.
{% endcomment %}
<form class="ot-filters" method="get" role="search" aria-label="Search {{ heading | downcase }} by case reference">
 <div class="ot-filters__grid">
 <div class="ot-field">
 <label for="f-ref">Case reference</label>
 <input type="search" id="f-ref" name="ref" value="{{ f_ref | escape }}" placeholder="Contains">
 </div>
 <div class="ot-field">
 <label for="f-route">Route</label>
 <select id="f-route" name="route">
 <option value="">Any</option>
 {% for r in routes.results.entities %}
 <option value="{{ r.al_reviewrouteid }}"{% if f_route == r.al_reviewrouteid %} selected{% endif %}>{{ r.al_name | escape }}</option>
 {% endfor %}
 </select>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div class="ot-filters__actions">
 <button type="submit" class="ot-btn ot-btn--primary">Search</button>
 {% if f_ref != '' or f_route != '' %}
 <a class="ot-btn" href="?">Clear</a>
 {% endif %}
 </div>
</form>

{% comment %}
 The reviewer's own reviews (AD-218). On the AQS page they are split into the three states
 AR-03 asks the portal to tell apart - allocated, in progress, completed - by the review's
 own status. The Tax page keeps one table. group_value 0 means "every status".

 A range and single-value `when`s rather than a split list: this site's Liquid rejects a
 multi-character split, and a multi-value `when` has silently never matched here before.
{% endcomment %}
{% if rows.size > 0 %}
 {% if is_aqs_queue %}
 {% assign group_first = 120910210 %}{% assign group_last = 120910212 %}
 {% else %}
 {% assign group_first = 0 %}{% assign group_last = 0 %}
 {% endif %}
 {% for group_value in (group_first..group_last) %}
 {% case group_value %}
 {% when 120910210 %}{% assign g_title = 'Allocated to me' %}{% assign g_id = 'ot-reviews-allocated' %}
 {% when 120910211 %}{% assign g_title = 'In progress' %}{% assign g_id = 'ot-reviews-progress' %}
 {% when 120910212 %}{% assign g_title = 'Completed' %}{% assign g_id = 'ot-reviews-completed' %}
 {% else %}{% capture g_title %}{{ heading }} allocated to you{% endcapture %}{% assign g_id = 'ot-reviews-heading' %}
 {% endcase %}
 {% assign g_count = 0 %}
 {% for r in rows %}{% if group_value == 0 or r.al_reviewstatus.value == group_value %}{% assign g_count = g_count | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}

 <section class="ot-card" aria-labelledby="{{ g_id }}">
 <h2 class="ot-card__title" id="{{ g_id }}">{{ g_title | escape }}</h2>
 {% if f_ref != '' or f_route != '' %}
 <p class="ot-filters__summary">
 Showing {{ heading | downcase }}{% if f_ref != '' %} whose case reference contains
 &ldquo;{{ f_ref | escape }}&rdquo;{% endif %}{% if f_route != '' %}{% if f_ref != '' %} and{% endif %}
 on the {% for r in routes.results.entities %}{% if r.al_reviewrouteid == f_route %}{{ r.al_name | escape }}{% endif %}{% endfor %} route{% endif %}.
 </p>
 {% endif %}

 {% if g_count > 0 %}
 <div class="ot-table-wrap">
 <table class="ot-table">
 <caption>{{ g_count }} of the {{ reviews.results.total_record_count }} reviews allocated to you, page {{ page_number }}.</caption>
 <thead>
 <tr>
 <th scope="col">Case reference</th>
 <th scope="col">Client</th>
 <th scope="col">Adviser</th>
 <th scope="col">Tax checker</th>
 <th scope="col">AQS checker</th>
 <th scope="col">Route</th>
 <th scope="col">Review</th>
 <th scope="col">Sequence</th>
 <th scope="col">Status</th>
 <th scope="col">Started</th>
 <th scope="col">Submitted</th>
 </tr>
 </thead>

 <tbody>
 {% for r in rows %}
 {% if group_value == 0 or r.al_reviewstatus.value == group_value %}
 <tr>
 <td>
 {% assign cid = r['case.al_outcomecaseid'] %}
 {% if cid %}
 <a href="/case-details?id={{ cid }}">
 {{ r['case.al_casereference'] | default: 'Untitled case' | escape }}
 </a>
 {% else %}
 {{ r['case.al_casereference'] | default: '—' | escape }}
 {% endif %}
 </td>
 <td>{{ r['case.al_clientname'] | escape }}</td>
 <td>{{ r['case.al_advisername'] | escape }}</td>
 {% comment %}Who each check is allocated to (project owner, 2026-09-24), as the case list shows it.{% endcomment %}
 {% capture tax_checker %}{{ r['case.al_taxcheckername'] }}{% endcapture %}
 {% capture aqs_checker %}{{ r['case.al_aqscheckername'] }}{% endcapture %}
 <td>{% if tax_checker != '' %}{{ tax_checker | escape }}{% else %}<span class="ot-checker">Not yet allocated</span>{% endif %}</td>
 <td>{% if aqs_checker != '' %}{{ aqs_checker | escape }}{% else %}<span class="ot-checker">Not yet allocated</span>{% endif %}</td>
 <td>{{ r['route.al_name'] | default: '—' | escape }}</td>
 <td><a href="/review?id={{ r.al_reviewinstanceid }}">{{ r.al_name | default: 'Open review' | escape }}</a></td>
 <td>{{ r.al_sequence | default: '—' }}</td>
 <td>{% include 'OT Status Badge' status: r.al_reviewstatus.label %}</td>
 <td>{{ r.al_startedon | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' | default: '—' }}</td>
 <td>{{ r.al_submittedon | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' | default: '—' }}</td>
 </tr>
 {% endif %}
 {% endfor %}

 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>
 {% else %}
 {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'None here' message: 'No review of yours on this page is in this state.' %}
 {% endif %}
 </section>
 {% endfor %}

<nav aria-label="Pagination" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
 {% if page_number > 1 %}
 <a href="?{{ scope_qs }}page={{ page_number | minus: 1 }}">&laquo; Previous</a>
 {% endif %}
 {% if rows.size == page_size %}
 <a href="?{{ scope_qs }}page={{ page_number | plus: 1 }}" style="margin-left: 1rem;">Next &raquo;</a>
 {% endif %}
</nav>

{% elsif f_ref != '' or f_route != '' %}
 {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'No review matches' message: 'No review allocated to you matches this search. Clear it to see all of yours.' %}
{% else %}
 {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'Nothing allocated to you' message: 'Reviews appear here once one is allocated to you, or once you pick a case up from the queue.' %}
{% endif %}

{% if user and can_claim %}
<script>
 {% comment %}/*
 * Picking a case up out of the shared queue (AD-076).
 *
 * Path: this page -> PATCH contact.al_claimrequest -> ClaimRequestPlugin ->
 * ClaimCasePlugin -> Dataverse.
 *
 * The browser no longer creates an al_caseassignment. It writes the case id onto the
 * signed-in contact's own row, and the comment above the request further down records
 * why that is the better boundary.
 *
 * The browser sends the case id and nothing else. The business code, the
 * Dataverse user, the review instance, the case status and the checker name on the case
 * header are all resolved server-side, and the plug-in refuses a case that is not in the
 * queue or that another checker has already taken.
 */{% endcomment %}
 (function () {
 'use strict';

 var buttons = document.querySelectorAll('[data-ot-claim]');
 if (!buttons.length) { return; }

 var contactId = '{{ user.id }}';
 var reviewType = '{{ reviewtype }}';
 var dialog = document.querySelector('[data-ot-claim-dialog]');
 var dialogCase = document.querySelector('[data-ot-claim-dialog-case]');
 var cancel = document.querySelector('[data-ot-claim-cancel]');
 var proceed = document.querySelector('[data-ot-claim-continue]');
 var status = document.querySelector('[data-ot-claim-status]');
 var pending = null;

 function show(message, kind) {
 if (!status) { return; }
 status.textContent = message;
 status.className = 'ot-response__status ot-response__status--' + (kind || 'info');
 status.setAttribute('role', kind === 'error' ? 'alert' : 'status');
 }

 {% comment %}/*
 * The server's own words, out of the response body.
 *
 * The body is an OData error object, so the message has to be read out of it rather than
 * scanned for in the raw text: a prefix found by indexOf and taken to the end of the
 * string carries the JSON tail with it.
 *
 * A body that will not parse is returned as it stands - it is still more than "something
 * went wrong" - and it is collapsed onto one line and capped, because an error line is
 * read on screen and the whole of a platform fault is not readable there.
 */{% endcomment %}
 function serverMessage(raw) {
 var text = String(raw || '').trim();
 if (!text) { return ''; }

 var message = text;
 try {
 var body = JSON.parse(text);
 var error = body && body.error ? body.error : body;
 if (error && error.innererror && error.innererror.message) {
 message = error.innererror.message;
 } else if (error && error.message) {
 message = error.message;
 }
 } catch (ignored) {
 {% comment %}// Not JSON. Keep the raw text.{% endcomment %}
 }

 message = String(message || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
 if (message.length > 300) { message = message.substring(0, 300) + '...'; }
 return message;
 }

 function friendlyError(raw, status) {
 var text = serverMessage(raw);
 var markers = ['PRECONDITION:', 'VALIDATION:', 'CONFLICT:', 'UNEXPECTED:'];
 for (var i = 0; i < markers.length; i += 1) {
 var at = text.indexOf(markers[i]);
 if (at !== -1) {
 return text.substring(at + markers[i].length).trim();
 }
 }

 {% comment %}/*
 * Nothing our code raised, so the platform refused before ClaimCasePlugin ran. This
 * used to answer "you may not have permission" for every such refusal, which sent
 * the last two investigations down a permissions path when the fault was a payload
 * the Web API could not parse - the wrong @odata.bind property names, answered 400.
 * A 400 is never a permission problem, and an association refusal carries a code
 * (90040105/90040106) that names the missing grant, so both are worth saying out
 * loud rather than flattening into one guess.
 */{% endcomment %}
 if (status === 400) {
 return 'The portal rejected the request before checking any permission, so nothing '
 + 'was assigned. This is a fault in the page rather than in your account - please '
 + 'report it.'
 + (text ? ' The server said: ' + text : '');
 }

 var code = /9004010[56]/.exec(text);
 if (code) {
 return 'This site is not allowed to link that case to your account (error ' + code[0]
 + '), so nothing was assigned. Your reviewer role is not the problem - please report it.';
 }

 if (status === 401 || status === 403) {
 return 'We could not pick that case up. You may not have permission to run checks.';
 }

 return 'That case was not picked up, and nothing was assigned. '
 + (text ? 'The server said: ' + text : 'The server gave no detail.')
 + (status ? ' (status ' + status + ')' : '');
 }

 function close() {
 if (dialog.open) { dialog.close(); }
 pending = null;
 }

 {% comment %}/*
 * The checklist this claim just created, opened straight away.
 *
 * A checker picks a case up in order to check it, so landing them back on the queue
 * costs a second hunt for the row they were already looking at. The review instance is
 * created downstream by ClaimCasePlugin, synchronously in the same write, so it exists
 * by the time the PATCH returns.
 *
 * This used to look the instance up over the Web API and reload the queue when that
 * failed. It always failed: the lookup selected and filtered columns that are not in the
 * Webapi/al_reviewinstance/fields allowlist - which governs reads as well as writes -
 * so every claim ended on a reloaded queue with "Opening the checklist..." never kept.
 * Widening that allowlist would let the assigned reviewer PATCH the review's status and
 * submitted-on, which the plug-ins are meant to own. So the page hands the case to the
 * review page instead, which resolves the open instance server-side in Liquid, where no
 * allowlist applies.
 */{% endcomment %}
 function openChecklist(caseId) {
 window.location.href = '/review?case=' + encodeURIComponent(caseId)
 + '&type=' + encodeURIComponent(reviewType);
 }

 function claim(button) {
 if (!window.shell || typeof window.shell.getTokenDeferred !== 'function') {
 show('This page did not load completely. Refresh and try again.', 'error');
 return;
 }

 button.disabled = true;
 show('Assigning the case to you...', 'info');

 {% comment %}/*
 * One text column on the checker's own contact row - not an al_caseassignment carrying
 * two @odata.bind lookups, which is what this used to send.
 *
 * Power Pages refused that association with 90040106 naming al_outcomecase, and went
 * on refusing it through three verified permission repairs: AppendTo on the case,
 * a contact permission, and the Tax and AQS Reviewer roles granted on both sides of
 * the association. That is the same refusal the answering module hit, and the same
 * resolution it reached (AD-053): the browser writes a trigger column and the server
 * does the association. ClaimRequestPlugin creates the assignment as the application
 * user, and ClaimCasePlugin then runs exactly as before.
 *
 * PATCHing your own contact is also a better boundary than the payload this replaces.
 * The site's contact permission is Self-scoped, so this request can only ever reach
 * the signed-in user's row, and the plug-in takes the claimant from the row it was
 * given rather than from anything sent here.
 */{% endcomment %}
 var payload = { al_claimrequest: button.getAttribute('data-case-id') };

 window.shell.getTokenDeferred().done(function (token) {
 var request = new XMLHttpRequest();
 request.open('PATCH', '/_api/contacts(' + contactId + ')', true);
 request.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
 request.setRequestHeader('__RequestVerificationToken', token);

 request.onreadystatechange = function () {
 if (request.readyState !== 4) { return; }

 if (request.status >= 200 && request.status < 300) {
 show('Assigned to you. Opening the checklist...', 'success');
 openChecklist(button.getAttribute('data-case-id'));
 return;
 }

 show(friendlyError(request.responseText, request.status), 'error');
 button.disabled = false;
 };

 request.send(JSON.stringify(payload));
 }).fail(function () {
 show('This page did not load completely. Refresh and try again.', 'error');
 button.disabled = false;
 });
 }

 for (var i = 0; i < buttons.length; i += 1) {
 buttons[i].addEventListener('click', function (event) {
 pending = event.currentTarget;
 dialogCase.textContent = pending.getAttribute('data-case-reference');

 {% comment %}// showModal gives the focus trap, the Escape key and the backdrop; where it is
 // unavailable the confirmation still has to happen, so fall back rather than
 // assigning the case with no warning at all.{% endcomment %}
 if (typeof dialog.showModal === 'function') {
 dialog.showModal();
 return;
 }

 if (window.confirm('You are about to start checks on ' + pending.getAttribute('data-case-reference')
 + '. The case will be assigned to you.')) {
 claim(pending);
 }
 pending = null;
 });
 }

 cancel.addEventListener('click', close);
 proceed.addEventListener('click', function () {
 var button = pending;
 close();
 if (button) { claim(button); }
 });
 })();
</script>
{% endif %}

<script>
 {% comment %}/* Working-day ages for the AQS queue (AR-03), on the same calendar as the remediation
 clock: Monday to Friday, the first day counts, bank holidays not deducted. */{% endcomment %}
 (function () {
 'use strict';
 function midday(text) {
 var parts = /^(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})$/.exec(text || '');
 return parts ? new Date(Date.UTC(+parts[1], +parts[2] - 1, +parts[3], 12, 0, 0)) : null;
 }
 function workingDaysBetween(from, to) {
 if (to.getTime() < from.getTime()) { return 0; }
 var count = 0;
 var cursor = new Date(from.getTime());
 while (cursor.getTime() <= to.getTime()) {
 var day = cursor.getUTCDay();
 if (day >= 1 && day <= 5) { count += 1; }
 cursor.setUTCDate(cursor.getUTCDate() + 1);
 }
 return count;
 }
 var now = new Date();
 var today = new Date(Date.UTC(now.getFullYear(), now.getMonth(), now.getDate(), 12, 0, 0));
 var cells = document.querySelectorAll('[data-ot-workdays]');
 for (var i = 0; i < cells.length; i += 1) {
 var from = midday(cells[i].getAttribute('data-from'));
 if (!from) { continue; }
 var age = workingDaysBetween(from, today);
 cells[i].textContent = age + (age === 1 ? ' working day' : ' working days');
 }
 })();
</script>
{% endif %}
" }
